{
  "id": "Compound::DB00635 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Prednisone is implicated in the pathogenesis of asthma through a complex network of molecular relationships. The evidence suggests that Prednisone, through its resemblance to Flunisolide and Methylprednisolone, shares a common pharmacological profile with corticosteroids, which are known to treat asthma. This is further supported by the fact that Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, directly treats asthma, and Prednisone is included in the same class of compounds. The recurring theme is the involvement of corticosteroid receptor agonists, which are central to the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids, and their potential to modulate inflammatory pathways associated with asthma. The integration of these relationships highlights the role of corticosteroid receptor activation in the therapeutic effects of Prednisone, suggesting a possible mechanism by which Prednisone may exert its effects on asthma. The evidence also points to a shared pathway involving 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, reinforcing the connection between Prednisone and the broader class of corticosteroids, which are known to influence inflammatory responses in respiratory diseases.",
